A||||4||mshill||Rear wheel bearing stress question....||||5||
Z||000000||mshill||12-16-2001||10:24 PM||||Here's a little background to the question: I am doing a Ferrari rebody and am trying to avoid using 3" spacers on the rear wheels and trying to avoid the $1500 that Held wants for the Wide Track Rear suspension.<P>Facts: Stock 86 Fiero Rims were 7" wide with a 35mm offset putting the backspacing at 3.5" (half the rim width) + 1.4" (35mm offset). So the stock rim puts about 5" of wheel inside the hub face and 2" outside the hub face. The toe in spec for the rear is 0 +/-0.2 degrees (essentially flat).<P>Here's the question: If I put 10 inch wide wheels, with zero offset,  it still puts 5 inches of wheel inside the hub face.  Why would this create a greater stress on the hub bearings if the wheel is effectively flat?<P>I understand that there is an extra 3 inches on the outside and that in a turn there may be additional load, but how is this different than real sticky tires?<P>I know there is a whole discussion on the widening of the suspension, but I am not going to put out all that money when it isn't absolutely necessary.||||reg||5
Z||000001||West Coast Fiero||12-16-2001||10:38 PM||||It seems as if you have done your homework. If the offset requirements are met, I can't see it being that much more significant in stress on the bearing. However if you have the wheels all ready then just bolt them on and find out - worst case scenario it will fail and you will have to buy the Held setup anyways<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://clubs.hemmings.com/fieroswest/WestCoast300.gif"> <BR>Eric Nelson<BR>Technician,<BR>West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000002||Jefrysuko||12-16-2001||11:57 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mshill:<BR><B>The toe in spec for the rear is 0 +/-0.2 degrees (essentially flat).</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually it is caster that you are thinking about.  Toe in is just as it sounds.  Look down at your feet and imagine them as the footprint of your tires.  With your feet parallel to each other there is 0 degrees toe in.  I think you can understand why you wouldn't want your rear tires anything than parallel.<P>Camber is the measurement that relates your tires to being perpendicular to the ground.  When you run bigger tires you might want to use a little less camber to relieve the stress on the bearings but this could cause other problems.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>I understand that there is an extra 3 inches on the outside and that in a turn there may be additional load, but how is this different than real sticky tires?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well by adding this extra width you effectively lengthen the moment arm in which torque is transmitted to the bearings.  The same thing occurs when using a larger diameter tire.  You effectively multiply the torque induced on the bearing.  The amount of multiplication is that of the new distance from the center of the bearing to the outer corner of the tire divided by the previous distance.<P>Is this multiplication more than that of running "sticky tires?"  I don't know the answer to that but I do know that people have experienced failures with "sticky tires" and also with increased wheel sizes as well as with stock setups.  In my opinion it would come down to how you plan on driving it.<P>Good luck,<BR>I hope some of this helped!||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||12-17-2001||12:10 AM||||I don't know which wheel you measured how.... the 14" wheels are 6" and +35. You measure inside to inside of the bead faces for rim width.<P><BR>A wide wheel like that should not be a problem....<P>the problem is when people use a big spacer to move a wheel out. Those can put most or all the wheel outboard of the hub bearing. not good. Plus most spacers suck. The spacer itself is as likely to fail as the hub.<P>The dif is that a wide wheel, or even a normal wheel with normal offsets, keeps the load more vertical on the hub. By normal offsets I mean the hub bearing is within the tire width.<P>A big spacer on the other hand... It's like sticking the wheel at the eraser end of a pencil when you're holding the point. You put a near constant prying load on the bearing.<P>Yes, the bearing normally gets a prying type load in a corner but it doesn't run that way constantly.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">   <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||mshill||12-17-2001||05:45 PM||||Anyone else want to weigh in on this?||||reg||1
